Q & A ()
1. What is the difference between high availability and fault tolerance? Which is best to strive for?
  High-availability systems are also lighter to set up as they are built to share resources with the aim of downtime minimization and failure co-management. In contrast, fault tolerance requires more hardware and software investment to detect failures and immediately switch to redundant components and power backups.
  High availability systems are designed to minimize downtime and avoid loss of service while Fault-tolerant systems are designed to go beyond high availability and ensure robust business continuity and, in some applications, even assist in disaster recovery.
  Highly available system that is not necessarily fault-tolerant would leverage a load balancer. Such a setup would allow for minimal downtime in service but lack total redundancy in case of failure.
  Fault-tolerant systems can be more expensive than high availability. This is because fault-tolerant systems demand the continuous operation and maintenance of redundant system components.
